issue_context.body:
Walking through a couple scenarios of how users would go about promoting their resources with the introduction of compatibility version. Feature gates are self explanatory via versioned_kube_features.go but resource APIs are a bit different. 

We determine the resources we serve based on the emulation version https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/master/pkg/controlplane/apiserver/apis.go#L91. Alpha APIs are disabled by default, and not compatible with emulation version so we can omit them from the current discussion. Beta APIs are also disabled by default, but is something we want to capture in emulation version. GA APIs are enabled by default, and is definitely something we want to capture in emulation version.

Promoting an API from alpha to beta and from beta to GA yields certain cases that need to be handled with emulation version.

User stories will be from the perspective of a feature developer. 

## User Story 1 : Promoting an API from alpha to beta.

Somewhere in the apiserver I have something like 

```go
// v1.31
if featuregate.Enabled(MyFeature) {
   r := &v1alpha1.MyType{}
   // operations on the resource
}
```

Once the feature goes beta, the traditional workflow will change the code to something like 

```go
// v1.32
if featuregate.Enabled(MyFeature) {
  // alpha resource directly replaced with beta resource on new version
   r := &v1beta1.MyType{}
   // operations on the resource
}
```

This is fine because we do not allow [alpha APIs to be enabled along with emulation version](https://github.com/kubernetes/enhancements/blob/master/keps/sig-architecture/4330-compatibility-versions/README.md#api-availability).  Thus, 1.32 can emulate 1.31 but `MyFeature` cannot be enabled, and despite not knowing about how to handle the alpha type when emulating 1.31, the code path will never be exercised. 

### User Story 2: Promoting feature from beta to GA

```go
// v1.32
if featuregate.Enabled(MyFeature) {
   r := &v1beta1.MyType{}
   // operations on the resource
}
```

```go
// v1.33
if featuregate.Enabled(MyFeature) {
   r := &v1.MyType{}
   // operations on the resource
}
```

Because of the [beta APIs off by default](https://github.com/kubernetes/enhancements/blob/master/keps/sig-architecture/3136-beta-apis-off-by-default/README.md), the feature gate will not be enabled by default in beta. However, let's say the API goes GA in 1.33. 1.33 cannot emulate 1.32 because the code operates on the GA types. v1 does not exist in 1.32's served versions so we cannot operate on it.

How would this be fixed? As a user would I need to perform something like this to satisfy compatibility version?

```go
// v1.33
if featuregate.Enabled(MyFeature) && featuregate.EmulationVersion() >= "1.33" {
  // alpha resource directly replaced with beta resource on new version
   r := &v1.MyType{}
   // operations on the resource
} else if  featuregate.Enabled(MyFeature) && featuregate.EmulationVersion() < "1.33" {
   r := &v1beta1.MyType{}
   // operations on the resource
}
```

This example is only for manipulation on a single type. If I'm writing a larger controller, would the entire controller code need to be duplicated and switched on emulation version? This seems like we're adding a bunch of extra work for beta to GA promotions (in addition to the complicated testing rules), and will need documentation if we pursue this path.

Alternatively, we can disallow emulation version to be set if beta features relying on beta APIs are enabled (they're off by default, so basically not allowing a default off feature to be set to default on under emulation version). Beta APIs are off by default due to stability risks and so we could mirror that with emulation version in not emulating unstable behavior. This will allow beta to GA transitions to be just like alpha to beta transitions in directly replacing the types. 

/sig api-machinery
/triage accepted

comments:
How could we enforce this for users deprecating a Beta API?  It seems we would need to have users do something like you suggest above IIUC:
```go
// v1.33
if featuregate.Enabled(MyFeature) && featuregate.EmulationVersion() >= "1.33" {
  // alpha resource directly replaced with beta resource on new version
   r := &v1.MyType{}
   // operations on the resource
} else if  featuregate.Enabled(MyFeature) && featuregate.EmulationVersion() < "1.33" {
   r := &v1beta1.MyType{}
   // operations on the resource
}
```

Would this be part of prerelease-lifecycle-gen or potentially a new linter?  Is there single "source of truth" for each release that states each API and it's release lifecycle? (For feature gates, this is somewhat non-trivial for past releases with examining a releases kube_features.go - https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/pull/127200#discussion_r1768862429)

> 
> to be more clear, the problem is not to emulate the APIs is to emulate the feature gates that depend on the APIs
> 
> Feature Gate	API	Binary Version	Emulated Version	Result
> GA	v1	1.33	-	ok
> Beta	v1beta1	1.33	1.32	controller depends on v1 and fails

Isn't the problem in this case that the controller should handle both versions of API, like @Jefftree described? 
```
// v1.33
if featuregate.Enabled(MyFeature) && featuregate.EmulationVersion() >= "1.33" {
  // alpha resource directly replaced with beta resource on new version
   r := &v1.MyType{}
   // operations on the resource
} else if  featuregate.Enabled(MyFeature) && featuregate.EmulationVersion() < "1.33" {
   r := &v1beta1.MyType{}
   // operations on the resource
}
```

If we do not allow emulating complex features like ServiceCIDR, it would violate our goal in the KEP to support emulating Beta features. And if the user starts to use one of these features, they would not be able to upgrade through the safe upgrade path (binary 1.32 -> [binary 1.33, emulated 1.32] -> [binary 1.33, emulated 1.33] , which means we would need to have a separate upgrade policy depending on the Beta features/APIs a cluster has turned on. 
Even if we can do that, we still need to handle the graduations of APIs not gated by a feature gate, which would still require adding code and wrap business logic.

> // v1.33
> if featuregate.Enabled(MyFeature) && featuregate.EmulationVersion() >= "1.33" {
>   // alpha resource directly replaced with beta resource on new version
>    r := &v1.MyType{}
>    // operations on the resource
> } else if  featuregate.Enabled(MyFeature) && featuregate.EmulationVersion() < "1.33" {
>    r := &v1beta1.MyType{}
>    // operations on the resource
> }
>  Isn't the problem in this case that the controller should handle both versions of API, like @Jefftree described?
> >  it would violate our goal in the KEP to support emulating Beta features

The KEP is to make upgrades safer based on the hypothesis that being able to emulate features we are able to make this safe path, but we are emulating business logic and duplicating code paths that can drift or have copy paste errors or will need backports ... debugging and troubleshooting will be really complex and will add a lot of overhead to the feature owners ... I'm skeptical that this approach will make the upgrade safer than with today's methid since we are "faking" the emulation and we may have copy paste errors ... I don't think there is enough return on investment

> Even if we can do that, we still need to handle the graduations of APIs not gated by a feature gate, which would still require adding code and wrap business logic.

AFAIK APIs are not feature gated and they should be using a stored version n-1 , so there should not be any need for handling the APIs
https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/6746df77f2376c6bc1fd0de767d2a94e6bd6cec1/pkg/kubeapiserver/default_storage_factory_builder.go#L63-L83

